## Verdict: cycle61's claim is **broken on all three of its specifics**, but "no residual" is also not demonstrable

Target: c61's `SL 決着後の取引固有残差 +0.0962 (z +3.19)`, judged `H_TRADE`, in-sample 2010-20.

**Gate first:** I reproduced c61's exact design in my own code at **|Δ(D)| = 0.0000, n identical (2121/4921)**. Every difference below is design, not implementation.

**What broke**

1. **Magnitude.** Widening the candidate pool 52 → 362 bars (all weekdays, same ET slot), using all in-tolerance candidates instead of nearest-1, and excluding real trade bars gives **+0.0593 (z 3.03)**. Ablation: pool −0.0405 / all-mean −0.0152 / trade-bar exclusion −0.0005. The drop is almost entirely on the **control** side (F' −0.0344 → +0.0137; the event side barely moves). c61's headline is a property of its 52-candidate same-weekday pool.
2. **c61's own decisive condition ①** (CEM-3) run at 7× power: **+0.0481 (z 1.57)** — fails c61's own `z ≥ 2.5` bar. Cluster sign-flip permutation **p 0.121**.
3. **"A 2010-20 property."** Sub-window adjusted residuals: 2010-12 **+0.0812 (z 2.90)**, then 2013-15 +0.016, 2016-18 +0.018, 2019-20 +0.034 (all |z| < 1). It's a **2010-12** phenomenon — D2's own logic applied inside c61's in-sample window.
4. **c61's alternative hypothesis (b), "the stab-and-recover wick shape," is refuted** — among zero-trade v-matched bars, the deep-vs-shallow wick gap is **+0.0073 (z 0.34)** against the +0.058 needed.

**New confound c61 never matched:** the run-in (`trail`, the 12 bars into the settlement bar) is off by **0.478R** (SL) / 0.756R (TP). It's a real effect (slope −0.0268, z −2.86, measured on zero-trade bars only) but explains just **23%**.

**My own attacks that failed, recorded as favouring c61:** look-ahead/horizon (relaxing the forward-continuity filter leaves D_adj at +0.039–+0.047, z ≈ 3.1), and "the control was another trade's bar" (ΔD −0.0005).

**The most important result is the one against my own conclusion.** My best estimator (regression-adjusted control, +0.0428 z 2.56, 99.6% *marginal* support) **fails its own placebo**: zero-trade pseudo-events in the event's covariate corner produce **+0.0273 (z 1.56)** — 64% of my reported figure. Joint support fails where marginal support looked fine. So "control artifact" and "tighter matching selects weaker events" cannot be separated with this pool, and I did **not** show the residual is zero.

Also: at a 0.20 USD round-trip spread the event's own F'_net is **−0.0296** (−0.1214 at 0.40) — nothing receivable under any design, confirming A7 under doubled cost. And across the 12 control designs ever applied to this cell, Holm passes exactly the **worst-calibrated** ones and fails every better-calibrated one, monotonically.

