## Target: cycle62 (18:40) — volatility-managed α

c62's main judgment was already null (C1 FAIL / C2 null, PROMOTED empty), so there was no promotion candidate to break. I attacked the five assertions holding that null up — and ran the decisive test **c62 itself registered but never executed**: a calibrated null simulation.

**Replication gate passed first** (α +0.334 / +1.773 / +5.866 vs c58c's +0.33 / +1.77 / +5.87, scaling constants identical), so every number is directly comparable.

### I broke my own null before reporting it
My first calibrated null resampled ε = (r−μ̂)/σ_real iid. Breaking the pairing injects `mean(σ_real)·mean(ε)` into the null drift, and mean(ε) = +0.077 ≠ 0 — which *is* the timing signal. The null was feeding itself the signal (μ_null +19.05 vs true +10.36), inflating the mechanical term +3.17 → +4.72 and the p-value 0.112 → 0.295. The bias pointed toward the conclusion I was about to publish. Fixed by centering the ε pool; all reported numbers are post-fix.

### Verdicts
| claim | verdict |
|---|---|
| main judgment (C1 FAIL / C2 null) | **survived, strengthened** |
| A1 "63% mechanical, residual = noise" | **survived**, share corrected 63% → **54%**; residual +2.70 bp = +1.29 null sd, p 0.098–0.112, Bonferroni(12) = 1.00 |
| A2 "circular-shift null is anti-conservative" | **confirmed** — perm centre −0.31 vs calibrated +3.17 = 2.0 perm sd too low |
| A3 "cost is not the barrier" | **survived**, but only for spread: 59% of α sits in the levered leg (w̄ 1.199, w_max 2.06, excess exposure 0.285 notional); w≤1 keeps just 41% |
| A4 "+14.8%/yr" | **broken** — scaling constant `c` is fitted inside the evaluation window; causal `c` gives **+10.89%/yr** (t unchanged) |
| A5 "trend-sign artefact" | **broken as a general mechanism** — US500/US100 go the *other* way, 4/4 cells, Stouffer z −2.16 (p 0.031) |

c62's own worst fear — "the mechanical term could collapse to the permutation null's 0 and the residual return to the full +5.9 bp" — is **broken**: with real forecast error it is +3.17, i.e. 86% of the closed form, not 0.

### New knowledge the cycle produced
On the daily grid, equity-index vol-management sits **below** the zero-timing null in 8/8 windows. c62 logged C1 FAIL as "power vs absence unseparated"; relative to the correct null the sign is decided — high-σ̂ periods are followed by *better* returns than constant drift predicts, so VM misses post-crash rebounds (consistent with Cederburg et al. 2020).

Also worth flagging against c62's framing: "single 2025 episode" doesn't hold up. Dropping 2025 cuts drift to 41% but leaves α +4.84 and an excess of +3.13 bp — tripping my own pre-registered rule D1 — and ρ(excess_y, μ_y) is still +0.67 (p 0.003) after removing the mechanical term. The excess averages to zero across years (Stouffer −0.09), so c62's direction holds, but the closed form explains only part of the phenomenon.

My own most serious counter-refutation, left standing: the excess has an MDE of ~4.2 bp/day, so a true +2.7 bp timing α is invisible to this test. "Survived" means *no detectable* timing information. Decisive condition registered: forward ≥250 sessions from 2026-07, pre-registering the excess-over-calibrated-null (not α) with excess > 0 ∧ z ≥ 2 ∧ raw SR < 1.

